is often a sensible choice.'' The paragraph lists the reasons and ends: \"less an act of carelessness than a rational response to the options on offer\". Spare parts are one detail; carelessness is the opposite of the writer''s point.\n2 A, ''The aim is to teach owners, not to sell them a service.'' Proof: \"The intention is educational rather than commercial\". The fee and the referrals are single details.\n3 A, ''For many people, the social side matters more than the mending.'' Proof: \"the repairs themselves are almost incidental\". The engineers are one example; the councils fund events \"partly on these grounds\", for the social benefits as much as for waste.\n4 C, ''Critics doubt how much good the cafés really do.'' Proof: \"Sceptics, however, question how much difference such events actually make.\" Safety is one detail; the passage says \"a few hundred items a year\", not thousands.\n5 C, ''Whether a product can be repaired is decided by its design, not by its owner.'' Proof: \"Whether a product can be repaired, they contend, is largely decided long before it reaches a consumer\". Paying for cafés is never suggested.\n6 A, ''Repair laws are changing, and the cafés may help make that possible.'' The paragraph moves from new regulations to what makes \"stricter rules politically achievable\". Supporters \"see no contradiction\", so the third option is the opposite.\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Distinguish main and supporting ideas: choose the statement that covers a whole paragraph."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: the main idea covers every sentence

A heading must fit the WHOLE paragraph. Test each option: could every sentence of the paragraph sit under it?
- A DETAIL fits one sentence only.
- A WRONG IDEA uses words from the paragraph but says something it does not say.
- The MAIN IDEA fits all of it. It is often in the first or last sentence.

WORKED EXAMPLE (Paragraph A)
Option 1: ''Repair cafés began with an event in Amsterdam.'' Only one sentence: a detail.
Option 2: ''Repair cafés have spread with no one in charge.'' The paragraph moves from one event to "thousands of locations", spread "without any central body directing it". Every sentence fits.  -> MAIN IDEA

Now choose the main idea for paragraphs B to G.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'67bd369b805c0f85373c227e18e06bf3aa84a97ecb72889fe3f792b2a4657d13',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
