: [{"value": "A", "label": "A  it fits the whole paragraph"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  too narrow: it covers only one detail"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the paragraph says the opposite"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  it belongs to a different paragraph"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Paragraph E, heading ''Doubts about the real effect''. This heading is ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "2b18f4c1-ed3c-4281-b352-8057fa69494b", "supporting_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  it fits the whole paragraph"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  too narrow: it covers only one detail"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the paragraph says the opposite"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  it belongs to a different paragraph"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Paragraph D, heading ''Why councils pay for repair events''. This heading is ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "2b18f4c1-ed3c-4281-b352-8057fa69494b", "supporting_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  it fits the whole paragraph"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  too narrow: it covers only one detail"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the paragraph says the opposite"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  it belongs to a different paragraph"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Paragraph F, heading ''New laws on spare parts''. This heading is ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "2b18f4c1-ed3c-4281-b352-8057fa69494b", "supporting_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  it fits the whole paragraph"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  too narrow: it covers only one detail"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the paragraph says the opposite"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  it belongs to a different paragraph"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 B, too narrow. Amsterdam is in one sentence: \"usually traced to a single event staged in Amsterdam in 2009\". The paragraph is about how the idea spread.\n2 A, it fits. Proof: \"The intention is educational rather than commercial\". Every sentence shows the owner learning.\n3 C, the opposite. Proof: \"they do not set out to undercut local repair businesses\". They send work to the shops instead.\n4 A, it fits. Proof: \"question how much difference such events actually make\". Scale, weak measurement and optimistic estimates are all doubts about the effect.\n5 B, too narrow. Councils appear only in the last sentence. The paragraph is about the social rewards: \"the repairs themselves are almost incidental\".\n6 D, the wrong paragraph. The laws are in G: \"new regulations oblige manufacturers to supply spare parts\". F is about design and responsibility.\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Distinguish main and supporting ideas: recognise why a tempting heading fails (too narrow, contradicted, wrong paragraph)."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: name the trap

Wrong headings are wrong in three main ways:
1. TOO NARROW: it matches one detail, not the whole paragraph.
2. THE OPPOSITE: it uses the paragraph''s topic but the text says the opposite.
3. WRONG PARAGRAPH: it is true of the passage, but not of this paragraph.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Paragraph B, heading ''The rising cost of new electrical goods''.
The paragraph says "Retail prices for many small appliances have, in real terms, actually fallen over recent decades".
Prices fell; the heading says they rose.  -> the paragraph says the opposite

Now judge six paragraph and heading pairs.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'2beb1cd43e91b214c12c6ebdcb19bbcab996d25a15aadce986320fc329ab3a31',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r42-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 9 step 3 of 6: What is the paragraph doing?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose what each paragraph does in the writer''s argument. Each answer is used once.','Read the first sentence and the signal words (however, sceptics, some commentators, that argument). They tell you the paragraph''s job.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Paragraph A ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "supporting_node_id": "7e4ffe5a-500d-4297-a16b-5b965fb1b705", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  reports criticism"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  explains a cause"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  reports a change and draws a conclusion"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  introduces the topic and how it spread"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  moves the responsibility to someone else"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  describes how something is done"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Paragraph B ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "supporting_node_id": "7e4ffe5a-500d-4297-a16b-5b965fb1b705", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  reports criticism"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  explains a cause"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  reports a change and draws a conclusion"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  introduces the topic and how it spread"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  moves the responsibility to someone else"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  describes how something is done"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Paragraph C ...", "accepted_answers": ["F"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "supporting_node_id": "7e4ffe5a-500d-4297-a16b-5b965fb1b705", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  reports criticism"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  explains a cause"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  reports a change and draws a conclusion"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  introduces the topic and how it spread"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  moves the responsibility to someone else"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  describes how something is done"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Paragraph E ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "supporting_node_id": "7e4ffe5a-500d-4297-a16b-5b965fb1b705", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  reports criticism"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  explains a cause"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  reports a change and draws a conclusion"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  introduces the topic and how it spread"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  moves the responsibility to someone else"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  describes how something is done"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Paragraph F ...", "accepted_answers": ["E"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "supporting_node_id": "7e4ffe5a-500d-4297-a16b-5b965fb1b705", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  reports criticism"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  explains a cause"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  reports a change and draws a conclusion"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  introduces the topic and how it spread"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  moves the responsibility to someone else"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  describes how something is done"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Paragraph G ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "supporting_node_id": "7e4ffe5a-500d-4297-a16b-5b965fb1b705", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  reports criticism"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  explains a cause"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  reports a change and draws a conclusion"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  introduces the topic and how it spread"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  moves the responsibility to someone else"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  describes how something is done"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 D, introduces the topic and how it spread: from \"a single event staged in Amsterdam\" to \"thousands of locations\".\n2 B, explains a cause: why repairable things are thrown away (\"restoring them appeared more troublesome than replacing them\").\n3 F, describes how something is done: \"The owner is expected to sit beside the volunteer\" and the volunteer \"narrates each step\".\n4 A, reports criticism: \"Sceptics, however, question how much difference such events actually make.\"\n5 E, moves the responsibility to someone else: \"responsibility has been placed on the wrong shoulders\", away from consumers and towards design.\n6 C, reports a change and draws a conclusion: \"That argument has begun to influence legislators\", then \"Their contribution, it seems, may lie less in the quantity of objects they save than in what they make visible\".\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Recognise text organisation: identify the function of each paragraph in an argument."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: every paragraph has a job

Paragraphs in an argument do different jobs: introduce, explain a cause, describe a method, report criticism, move responsibility, conclude.
Signal words show the job:
- ''however'', ''sceptics'': criticism or a contrast is coming
- ''some commentators go further'': a stronger criticism
- ''that argument'', ''it seems'': linking back, drawing a conclusion

WORKED EXAMPLE (Paragraph D)
First sentence: "For many regular attendees, however, the repairs themselves are almost incidental."
''However'' turns away from the repairs. The rest of D describes conversations, feeling useful and confidence.
Job: it adds a different kind of benefit. (D is the example, so it is not one of the questions.)

Now choose the job of the other six paragraphs.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'453826f668ec6f34dca9c7f2ff460cdb2e89dc4e9ed343e0ec308a7b95299117',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r43-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 9 step 4 of 6: Follow the links','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose what each linking phrase refers to.','A linking phrase points back to something already said. Find it in the sentences just before, and check it is the whole idea, not one word.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "In paragraph B, ''Seen in this light'' refers to ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fd1c70d0-1cde-4423-b18c-b2d9fa5842b3", "supporting_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  the views of local repair shops"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  all the costs and obstacles the paragraph has just listed"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the carelessness of people who throw things away"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  only the falling price of new appliances"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "In paragraph C, ''on the contrary'' introduces ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fd1c70d0-1cde-4423-b18c-b2d9fa5842b3", "supporting_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  the reason the cafés charge a fee"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  evidence that the cafés help local repair businesses"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  a complaint from local repair businesses"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  a warning about safety"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "In paragraph D, ''on these grounds'' refers to ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fd1c70d0-1cde-4423-b18c-b2d9fa5842b3", "supporting_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  the social benefits the paragraph has just described"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the cost of running a repair event"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the amount of waste the cafés save"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  the skills of retired engineers"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "In paragraph E, ''such data'' means information about ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fd1c70d0-1cde-4423-b18c-b2d9fa5842b3", "supporting_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  how many volunteers each café has"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  how much carbon each repair saved"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  how many people visit each event"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  whether repaired items were still being used later"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "In paragraph F, ''From this perspective'' means from the point of view of ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fd1c70d0-1cde-4423-b18c-b2d9fa5842b3", "supporting_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  the manufacturers who make the products"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the commentators who say repairability is decided by design"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the volunteers who run the cafés"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  the consumers who throw things away"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "In paragraph G, ''That argument'' refers to the idea that ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fd1c70d0-1cde-4423-b18c-b2d9fa5842b3", "supporting_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  repairability is decided at the design stage"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  repair cafés make little difference to waste"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  councils should fund repair cafés"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  volunteers'' time and skills are limited"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 B, all the costs and obstacles: unobtainable parts, \"professional labour, charged by the hour\", products \"glued, welded or secured with unusual screws\", and falling prices. Falling prices alone is too narrow.\n2 B, evidence that the cafés help local repair businesses: \"items requiring specialist equipment are often referred to them\". It contrasts with the idea of undercutting them.\n3 A, the social benefits: conversations between neighbours, \"the satisfaction of feeling useful\" and new confidence. The councils fund events partly for these reasons, not only for waste.\n4 D, whether repaired items were still being used later. Organisers rarely record \"whether it was still in use six months later\". ''Such data'' points back to this missing information.\n5 B, the commentators who say repairability is decided by design: \"Whether a product can be repaired, they contend, is largely decided long before it reaches a consumer\".\n6 A, repairability is decided at the design stage, the argument of paragraph F. The laws respond to it by controlling design: \"avoid designs that obstruct repair\". The doubts about difference are paragraph E.\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Track reference: follow linking phrases back to the ideas they refer to."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: look back to find the link

Writers join ideas with phrases that point back: ''this'', ''such'', ''these grounds'', ''that argument'', ''from this perspective''.
To understand the argument, you must know what each one points to.
1. Read the sentence or two BEFORE the phrase.
2. Choose the idea that makes sense in its place. Try replacing the phrase with it.
3. Check it is the WHOLE idea, not one word that happens to be nearby.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Paragraph A: "Notably, this expansion has taken place without any central body directing it".
''This expansion'' points back to the sentence before: the event "has since been replicated in thousands of locations across several continents".
So ''this expansion'' = the copying of the event in thousands of places.

Now follow six links.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'bc1a1df08479e032a0381442fd3090a94cc720835ee41ca8084e07dd89652587',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r44-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 9 step 5 of 6: What does the writer really say?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the answer that matches the passage exactly, not one that goes further.','Reject answers that are stronger, wider or more certain than the passage. The right answer says what the text says, no more.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "According to paragraph A, repair cafés ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  grew from one event into a movement that no one controls"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  are run by an international organisation"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  were started by local councils"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  are found mainly in Amsterdam"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "In paragraph C, the organisers'' attitude to local repair businesses is ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  competitive"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  uninterested"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  cooperative"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  critical"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "In paragraph D, the writer says that visitors come to believe that ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  new products are always better than old ones"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the objects around them can be understood, not just bought and thrown away"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  councils ought to pay them for attending"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  they should train as professional repairers"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "In paragraph E, the critics'' main point about the number of repairs is that ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  it is falling every year"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  it is higher than organisers claim"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  it is very small compared with the waste a town produces"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  it is impossible to count"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "In paragraph F, the commentators suggest that repair cafés may ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  increase the amount of waste"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  be paid for by manufacturers"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  need to be closed down"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  help manufacturers avoid responsibility for waste"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "According to paragraph G, the new regulations require manufacturers to ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  give money to repair cafés"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  supply parts and manuals for a set number of years"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  stop selling products that use screws"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  repair every faulty product themselves"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 9, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 A, grew from one event into a movement that no one controls: \"What began as a one-off experiment has since been replicated in thousands of locations\", \"without any central body directing it\".\n2 C, cooperative: \"they do not set out to undercut local repair businesses; on the contrary, items requiring specialist equipment are often referred to them\".\n3 B, objects can be understood: \"the objects around them can be understood rather than merely purchased and discarded\".\n4 C, it is very small compared with the waste a town produces: a café \"may repair a few hundred items a year, a figure that barely registers against the tonnes of electrical and textile waste a single town generates\".\n5 D, help manufacturers avoid responsibility: cafés \"may even allow manufacturers to present waste as a problem of consumer behaviour rather than of product design\". ''May'' matters: it is a possibility, not a fact.\n6 B, supply parts and manuals for a set number of years: \"supply spare parts and repair manuals for a minimum number of years\".\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Identify viewpoint: choose the statement that matches what the writer and the people quoted actually say, rejecting overstatements."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: no more than the text says

Multiple-choice options often take a true idea and push it too far:
- STRONGER: ''question how much difference'' becomes ''make no difference''.
- WIDER: ''some commentators'' becomes ''everyone''.
- MORE CERTAIN: ''may'' becomes ''will''.
The right answer matches the text''s strength exactly.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Question: In paragraph E, what do the critics say about the carbon estimates?
Too strong: ''The estimates are completely false.''
Matches: ''The estimates are probably too high.'' Proof: "estimates of the carbon saved rest on assumptions that critics regard as optimistic". Optimistic = probably too high.

Now choose six answers that say no more than the text.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'9a20b7d54167b3f1205716c3ae00e3f806d7a5a5f7b0cd11b79770a3c0965ca3',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r45-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 9 step 6 of 6: Headings check','reading','independent_check','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the correct heading for paragraphs B to G from the list. There are more headings than paragraphs. Work on your own, without notes, a dictionary or help. Tell your teacher if anything interrupted you.','Use the whole routine: find the main idea, test the heading against every sentence, and name the trap in the headings you reject.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Paragraph B", "accepted_answers": ["iv"], "taxonomy_node_id": "2b18f4c1-ed3c-4281-b352-8057fa69494b", "supporting_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "options": [{"value": "i", "label": "i  Questions about the real impact"}, {"value": "ii", "label": "ii  A growing market for professional repairers"}, {"value": "iii", "label": "iii  Fix the design, not the object"}, {"value": "iv", "label": "iv  A sensible choice, not a careless one"}, {"value": "v", "label": "v  Lawmakers respond"}, {"value": "vi", "label": "vi  How councils measure waste"}, {"value": "vii", "label": "vii  More than mending"}, {"value": "viii", "label": "viii  Learning at the repair table"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Paragraph C", "accepted_answers": ["viii"], "taxonomy_node_id": "2b18f4c1-ed3c-4281-b352-8057fa69494b", "supporting_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "options": [{"value": "i", "label": "i  Questions about the real impact"}, {"value": "ii", "label": "ii  A growing market for professional repairers"}, {"value": "iii", "label": "iii  Fix the design, not the object"}, {"value": "iv", "label": "iv  A sensible choice, not a careless one"}, {"value": "v", "label": "v  Lawmakers respond"}, {"value": "vi", "label": "vi  How councils measure waste"}, {"value": "vii", "label": "vii  More than mending"}, {"value": "viii", "label": "viii  Learning at the repair table"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Paragraph D", "accepted_answers": ["vii"], "taxonomy_node_id": "2b18f4c1-ed3c-4281-b352-8057fa69494b", "supporting_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "options": [{"value": "i", "label": "i  Questions about the real impact"}, {"value": "ii", "label": "ii  A growing market for professional repairers"}, {"value": "iii", "label": "iii  Fix the design, not the object"}, {"value": "iv", "label": "iv  A sensible choice, not a careless one"}, {"value": "v", "label": "v  Lawmakers respond"}, {"value": "vi", "label": "vi  How councils measure waste"}, {"value": "vii", "label": "vii  More than mending"}, {"value": "viii", "label": "viii  Learning at the repair table"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Paragraph E", "accepted_answers": ["i"], "taxonomy_node_id": "2b18f4c1-ed3c-4281-b352-8057fa69494b", "supporting_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "options": [{"value": "i", "label": "i  Questions about the real impact"}, {"value": "ii", "label": "ii  A growing market for professional repairers"}, {"value": "iii", "label": "iii  Fix the design, not the object"}, {"value": "iv", "label": "iv  A sensible choice, not a careless one"}, {"value": "v", "label": "v  Lawmakers respond"}, {"value": "vi", "label": "vi  How councils measure waste"}, {"value": "vii", "label": "vii  More than mending"}, {"value": "viii", "label": "viii  Learning at the repair table"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Paragraph F", "accepted_answers": ["iii"], "taxonomy_node_id": "2b18f4c1-ed3c-4281-b352-8057fa69494b", "supporting_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "options": [{"value": "i", "label": "i  Questions about the real impact"}, {"value": "ii", "label": "ii  A growing market for professional repairers"}, {"value": "iii", "label": "iii  Fix the design, not the object"}, {"value": "iv", "label": "iv  A sensible choice, not a careless one"}, {"value": "v", "label": "v  Lawmakers respond"}, {"value": "vi", "label": "vi  How councils measure waste"}, {"value": "vii", "label": "vii  More than mending"}, {"value": "viii", "label": "viii  Learning at the repair table"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Paragraph G", "accepted_answers": ["v"], "taxonomy_node_id": "2b18f4c1-ed3c-4281-b352-8057fa69494b", "supporting_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "options": [{"value": "i", "label": "i  Questions about the real impact"}, {"value": "ii", "label": "ii  A growing market for professional repairers"}, {"value": "iii", "label": "iii  Fix the design, not the object"}, {"value": "iv", "label": "iv  A sensible choice, not a careless one"}, {"value": "v", "label": "v  Lawmakers respond"}, {"value": "vi", "label": "vi  How councils measure waste"}, {"value": "vii", "label": "vii  More than mending"}, {"value": "viii", "label": "viii  Learning at the repair table"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 iv. The paragraph explains why throwing things away is reasonable: \"less an act of carelessness than a rational response\".\n2 viii. The owner learns at the table: \"The intention is educational rather than commercial\". Heading ii is a trap: the cafés do not train professionals.\n3 vii. The rewards go beyond repairs: \"the repairs themselves are almost incidental\".\n4 i. Critics \"question how much difference such events actually make\". Heading vi is a trap: the paragraph is about how organisers record repairs, not councils measuring waste.\n5 iii. Repairability is decided \"at the design stage\", so mending objects one at a time \"treats the symptom rather than the cause\".\n6 v. \"That argument has begun to influence legislators\": new regulations on spare parts and design.\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Matching headings on a full argument, with decoy headings that match one detail, a related idea or no paragraph at all."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: the whole routine

1. Read the paragraph and say its main idea in a few words.
2. Find the heading with the same meaning. Test it against EVERY sentence.
3. Reject the traps: too narrow, the opposite, or the wrong paragraph.
There are more headings than paragraphs, so two headings are not used.

WORKED EXAMPLE (Paragraph A)
Main idea: one event became a movement with no one in charge.
A heading such as ''An idea that spread without central direction'' would fit. (Paragraph A is the example, so it is not one of the questions.)

Now match paragraphs B to G on your own.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'bd075e1feb9b4c424263fc5e1354dc7bf8836f0fa1826bbdb1f7867d25f38d3c',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
end $steps$;
